      "text": "Given the polynomial equation \\(x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x = 9\\), we want to compute \\(\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s}\\), where \\(r\\), \\(s\\), and \\(t\\) are the roots of the polynomial. To find the roots of the polynomial, we first rewrite it as:\n\n\\[x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = 0.\\]\n\nWe can use the Rational Root Theorem to test possible rational roots, which are the factors of the constant term \\(-9\\) (namely \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 9\\)). By testing these values, we find that \\(x = 3\\) is a root:\n\n\\[3^3 - 5 \\cdot 3^2 + 6 \\cdot 3 - 9 = 27 - 45 + 18 - 9 = 0.\\]\n\nSo, \\(x = 3\\) is indeed a root. We can factor \\(x - 3\\) out of the polynomial using synthetic division:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n 3 & 1 & -5 & 6 & -9 \\\\\n   &   & 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n   & 1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe quotient is \\(x^2 - 2x - 3\\), so we can write the equation as:\n\n\\[x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = (x - 3)(x^2 - 2x - 3).\\]\n\nNext, we factorize \\(x^2 - 2x - 3\\):\n\n\\[x^2 - 2x - 3 = (x - 3)(x + 1).\\]\n\nTherefore, the polynomial can be factored completely as:\n\n\\[x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = (x - 3)^2(x + 1).\\]\n\nThe roots are \\(x = 3\\) (with multiplicity 2) and \\(x = -1\\). Thus, the roots \\(r\\), \\(s\\), and \\(t\\) are \\(3\\), \\(3\\), and \\(-1\\) in some order. In all possible permutations, we have \\(r = 3\\), \\(s = 3\\), \\(t = -1\\) or \\(r = 3\\), \\(s = -1\\), \\(t = 3\\) or \\(r = -1\\), \\(s = 3\\), \\(t = 3\\). The value we will get is the same for any permutation, so we can choose one such permutation for simplicity. Now, we can find the desired expression. Using any permutation we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s} = \\frac{3 \\cdot (-1)}{3} + \\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{3} + \\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{3} = -1 + 9 + 3 = 11.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}.\n\\]",
