      "text": "Let $u=\\frac{rs}t$, $v=\\frac{st}r$, and $w=\\frac{tr}s$. We want to compute $u+v+w$.\nWe have the equation $x=g\\left(\\lambda x\\right)$ and a solution for $x$. First, we divide by $w$:\n$$\n1-\\frac vw = \\frac{x-vw}{w}.\n$$\nSince $x$ is a solution, we know that $1-\\frac vw$ is another root.- then $\\frac{rs}t + \\frac{st}r + \\frac{tr}s = uvw = \\boxed{27}.$\n\nWrong. Solution: We computed $uvw$.\n\nTo correct my mistake, we compute $u+v+w$.\nObserve that $\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s} = \\left(\\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s}\\right) + \\frac{tr}{s} + \\frac{st}{r} = \\frac{ss + rr + ss + sr + st + sr + ss + st}{rr + ss + tt}.$\nThen\n\\[\nu+v+w =\n\\frac{u+v+u+v+u+v}{u+v+u+v+u+v} = \\frac{3(u+v+u+v)}{u+v+u+v+u+v} = \\frac{6(u+v)}{u+v+3(u+v)} = \\frac{6}{4} = \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n\\]The answer is \\frac{3}{2}",
